producing systematic bias that sample size alone does not remove."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.12","skill":"2.A","q":"A telephone survey randomly samples registered voters, but only 38% of those contacted complete the interview. Which concern is most relevant?","correct":"Potential nonresponse bias if respondents and nonrespondents differ in opinion","distractors":["Undercoverage because no sampled voter had a chance to respond","Response bias caused solely by using a random sample","Sampling variability disappears because fewer people responded"],"e":"Low response creates potential nonresponse bias when respondents differ systematically from nonrespondents on the variable of interest. Random selection does not eliminate this post-selection problem."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.13","skill":"2.B","q":"A company tests two package designs. Within each of 20 stores, customers are randomly assigned to see Design A or Design B, and purchase rate is recorded. Why block by store?","correct":"Blocking store can reduce variation caused by store-to-store differences.","distractors":["Blocking makes the customers representative of consumers outside the sampled stores.","Blocking by store is sufficient by itself to establish causation.","Blocking makes random assignment within each store unnecessary."],"e":"Store characteristics can influence the response. Blocking separates that source of variation, while random assignment within each store supports a causal comparison of designs."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.13","skill":"2.B","q":"In a randomized experiment on a pain treatment, neither participants nor the clinicians evaluating pain know which treatment each participant receives. What is the primary purpose of this double masking?","correct":"To reduce bias from expectations of participants and evaluators","distractors":["To make the treatment groups representative of the national population","To ensure exactly equal sample sizes in the treatment groups","To replace random assignment with a stronger form of control"],"e":"Masking reduces the possibility that expectations influence reported or assessed responses. It does not create population representativeness and does not substitute for random assignment."},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.1","skill":"4.A","q":"Using the commuter table, which comparison provides the clearest evidence about whether departure time and transit use are associated?","correct":"Compare the conditional transit-use percentages within the two departure-time groups.","distractors":["Compare the two marginal departure-time percentages only.","Compare the grand total 400 with the transit total 116.","Compare the raw counts 72 and 176 without accounting for different row totals."],"e":"Association between two categorical variables is assessed by comparing conditional distributions. The relevant transit-use rates are 72/180 = 40% before 7:00 and 44/220 = 20% later, a substantial difference.","stimulusKey":"prob1","statsSetType":"probability"},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.2","skill":"3.B","q":"From the commuter table, what proportion of commuters who departed before 7:00 a.m. used public transit?","correct":"0.40","distractors":["72/400 = 0.18","116/180 ≈ 0.644","116/400 = 0.29"],"e":"This is a conditional relative frequency within the before-7:00 row: 72/180 = 0.40. Dividing by the grand total would instead give the joint relative frequency 72/400 = 0.18.","stimulusKey":"prob1","statsSetType":"probability"},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.6","skill":"3.C","q":"A commuter is selected at random from the table. Given that the commuter used public transit, what is the probability the trip began before 7:00 a.m.?","correct":"72/116, about 0.621","distractors":["72/180, or 0.400, using all before-policy riders as the denominator","72/400, or 0.180","116/400, or 0.290"],"e":"Conditioning on public transit restricts the sample space to the 116 transit users. Of those, 72 departed before 7:00, so the conditional probability is 72/116 ≈ 0.621.","stimulusKey":"prob1","statsSetType":"probability"},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.1","skill":"4.B","q":"In a two-way table, the conditional distribution of preferred payment method is nearly identical for customers under 40 and customers 40 or older. Which conclusion is most appropriate?","correct":"Little evidence of association between age group and payment preference","distractors":["Age group causes customers to choose the same payment method.","The variables are mutually exclusive